# Mobile Parity Rule — Kyarafit

## Purpose

Ensure Kyarafit remains a **single coherent product across web and mobile**, not two diverging implementations.

Mobile is not a secondary client.
It is a **first-class platform** that must evolve in lockstep with the web app.

---

## Core Rule

Any change to the web app that affects:

* features
* UI
* flows
* domain logic
* data models
* API behavior
* derived/computed values
* user-visible behavior

**must also include a corresponding mobile implementation or update in the same workstream.**

No exceptions unless explicitly documented.

---

## Default Assumption

Every change is:

> **Web + Mobile**

If something is truly platform-specific, it must be explicitly labeled:

* `web-only` (rare)
* `mobile-only` (rare)

Silence = parity required.

---

## What Requires Mobile Updates

The following ALWAYS require mobile consideration:

### 1. New Features

* Any new product capability added to web must exist on mobile
* Mobile may adapt UI, but must preserve behavior

### 2. UI / UX Changes

* Layout changes
* New components
* Interaction changes
* Navigation changes
* Information hierarchy changes

### 3. Domain & Data Changes

* Schema updates
* New fields or relationships
* Derived values (progress, cost, grouping, etc.)
* Validation rules

### 4. Behavior Changes

* Business logic updates
* Filtering / sorting logic
* Status transitions
* Automation or computed behavior

### 5. Shared Components / Systems

* Design system updates
* Shared hooks/utilities
* API contract changes

---

## Acceptable Differences (Important)

Mobile does NOT need to copy web UI exactly.

Allowed differences:

* stacked layouts instead of multi-column
* tabs instead of sidebars
* drill-down screens instead of dense views
* bottom sheets instead of modals
* simplified but equivalent interactions

**Rule: Behavior must match, presentation may adapt.**

---

## Explicit Non-Parity (Rare)

If mobile will NOT implement something:

You MUST document:

1. Why it is web-only
2. Why mobile cannot reasonably support it
3. What the fallback behavior is on mobile

This must be included in:

* PR description
* or a tracked follow-up issue

---

## Development Workflow Rules

### Ticket Creation

Every ticket must specify:

* `platform: web | mobile | both`

Default:

> `platform: both`

---

### Implementation Rule for Agents

When implementing a feature:

1. Identify if it impacts shared product behavior
2. If yes:

   * implement web version
   * implement mobile version
3. If mobile UI differs:

   * adapt UX
   * preserve behavior
4. Do NOT leave parity gaps for later unless explicitly tracked

---

### Pull Request Checklist

Every PR must answer:

* [ ] Does this change affect product behavior or UI?
* [ ] Does mobile need to be updated?
* [ ] Has the mobile implementation been included?
* [ ] If not, is there a documented reason?
* [ ] Were shared types/models updated for both platforms?
* [ ] Are there any parity gaps introduced?

PRs that introduce silent parity drift should be rejected.

---

## Architecture Guidance

To make parity sustainable:

* Keep **domain logic shared** where possible
* Keep **types/models consistent**
* Avoid duplicating business logic across platforms
* Prefer shared abstractions for:

  * validation
  * derived values
  * transformations
* Allow UI to diverge, not behavior

---

## Anti-Patterns (Do NOT do this)

* Shipping web features without mobile consideration
* Letting mobile lag “temporarily” without tracking
* Copy-pasting web UI blindly into mobile
* Reimplementing business logic differently per platform
* Treating mobile as a simplified version of the product

---

## Enforcement for AI Agents (Cursor)

When working on this repository:

* Always assume parity is required
* Before implementing a feature:

  * ask: “What is the mobile equivalent?”
* After implementing:

  * confirm parity exists
* If unsure:

  * default to implementing both
* Never ignore mobile unless explicitly instructed

---

## Success Criteria

This rule is working when:

* Web and mobile feel like the same product
* Features launch simultaneously across platforms
* No backlog of “mobile catch-up work”
* Users can switch platforms without losing capability
* Codebases share logic instead of drifting

---

## Short Version (for quick reference)

> If you change web, you change mobile.
> Same behavior, adapted UI.
> No silent drift.
